---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

// (a) Legacy shape. y: 100% at 60, 0% at 240 (1.8 units per percent).
const yOf = (m: number) => 240 - m * 180;
const pow2out = (t: number) => 1 - (1 - t) * (1 - t);
const LX = { spin: 70, drop: 180, dropEnd: 264, hold: 420, delay: 492, out: 594, end: 690 };
const legacyDrop = Array.from({ length: 11 }, (_, k) => {
  const t = k / 10;
  return [LX.drop + (LX.dropEnd - LX.drop) * t, yOf(1 - 0.7 * pow2out(t))] as [number, number];
});
const legacyPts: [number, number][] = [
  [LX.spin, yOf(1)], [LX.drop, yOf(1)], ...legacyDrop, [LX.hold, yOf(0.3)], [LX.delay, yOf(0.3)],
  [LX.out, yOf(0.3)], [LX.out, yOf(0)], [LX.end, yOf(0)],
];
const legacyPath = legacyPts.map(([x, y], i) => `${i === 0 ? 'M' : 'L'} ${x.toFixed(1)} ${y.toFixed(1)}`).join(' ');

// (b) Curve shape: three segments at 0.38 units per ms. y: 0% at 240, 100% at 120.
const yC = (m: number) => 240 - m * 120;
const pow2inout = (t: number) => (t < 0.5 ? 2 * t * t : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * t + 2, 2) / 2);
type Seg = { name: string; speed: number; duration: number; hold: number };
const segs: Seg[] = [
  { name: 'surge', speed: 1.5, duration: 150, hold: 100 },
  { name: 'crawl', speed: 0.15, duration: 350, hold: 250 },
  { name: 'final', speed: 0.3, duration: 150, hold: 300 },
];
const PX = 0.38;
let cx0 = 140;
let cur = 1;
const curvePts: [number, number][] = [[70, yC(1)], [140, yC(1)]];
const segMarks: { x: number; xEnd: number; xHold: number; seg: Seg; from: number }[] = [];
for (const seg of segs) {
  const from = cur;
  const xEnd = cx0 + seg.duration * PX;
  for (let k = 1; k <= 10; k++) {
    const t = k / 10;
    curvePts.push([cx0 + (xEnd - cx0) * t, yC(from + (seg.speed - from) * pow2inout(t))]);
  }
  const xHold = xEnd + seg.hold * PX;
  curvePts.push([xHold, yC(seg.speed)]);
  segMarks.push({ x: cx0, xEnd, xHold, seg, from });
  cx0 = xHold;
  cur = seg.speed;
}
curvePts.push([cx0, yC(0)], [cx0 + 40, yC(0)]);
const curvePath = curvePts.map(([x, y], i) => `${i === 0 ? 'M' : 'L'} ${x.toFixed(1)} ${y.toFixed(1)}`).join(' ');

// (c) Stagger modes: three stacked panels on one time scale, 0.29 units per ms.
const S = 0.29;
const TEASE = 450;
const STOP = 250;
const LANE_X0 = 70;
const LANE_W = 614;
const panels = [
  { title: 'stagger: 200', note: 'order * 200 ms', offsets: [0, 200, 400] },
  { title: 'stagger: [0, 100, 500]', note: 'arr[order] ?? 0', offsets: [0, 100, 500] },
  { title: "stagger: 'sequential'", note: 'waits for the previous tease reel to land', offsets: [0, TEASE + STOP, 2 * (TEASE + STOP)] },
];
const PANEL_H = 164;
const PANEL_Y = (i: number) => 60 + i * (PANEL_H + 12);

// (d) Slowdown interpolation example.
const slow = { from: 0.5, to: 0.2, holdFrom: 1, holdTo: 1.6 };
const count = 3;
const orders = [0, 1, 2].map((order) => {
  const f = count === 1 ? 0 : order / (count - 1);
  const mult = slow.from + (slow.to - slow.from) * f;
  const hold = slow.holdFrom + (slow.holdTo - slow.holdFrom) * f;
  return { order, f, mult, hold, duration: Math.round(450 * hold) };
});
---
<Architecture
  title="Anticipation"
  subtitle="The hesitation before a big reveal: how a reel slows, holds and creeps onto its symbols."
  eyebrow="Tension"
>
  <p>
    Two scatter symbols have landed and a third would trigger the bonus. The
    remaining columns do not drop the way they usually do. They slow to a
    crawl, hold there for half a second while the music swells, and then inch
    onto their symbols one cell at a time. That hesitation is
    <strong>anticipation</strong>, or a <strong>tease</strong>, and it is one of
    the most important pieces of feel in a slot. Done well it makes a near
    miss exciting; done badly it looks like lag.
  </p>

  <p>
    In pixi-reels a tease is a per-spin list of reels that slow down and hold
    before they stop. You name the reels with <code>setAnticipation()</code>
    after <code>spin()</code>; the controller slots an anticipation phase into
    each of those reels' chains, between the group barrier and the stop; and
    the stop phase that follows creeps onto the result at the tease speed
    instead of snapping back to full speed. This page covers the shape of the
    slowdown, how several teasing reels are spaced out, how a tease survives a
    skip press, and how the stop takes over at the end. ADR 020
    (<code>docs/adr/020-anticipation-feel.md</code>) records why the curve
    looks the way it does.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    A tease is three things the player feels in a row: the drop (the reel
    visibly brakes), the hold (it crawls at a fixed slow speed), and the creep
    (it stays slow while the result symbols roll in). Everything on this page
    is a knob on one of those three.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The default shape</h2>

  <p>
    Figure 1 plots a teasing reel's speed over time on the NORMAL profile,
    with the phases it passes through underneath. Follow the line from the
    left. It runs at full speed, brakes sharply to 30% over the first third of
    the hold, stays there for the rest of it, keeps that speed through the
    stop delay, and then through the spin-out as well, until the reel lands
    and bounces. The two dashed guides are the clamp the spin-out applies:
    whatever speed the tease leaves behind is kept, but never below 8% or
    above 100% of <code>spinSpeed</code>.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={300} caption="The legacy shape on NORMAL. Speed drops to 30% over the first 35% of the hold with power2.out, holds for the remaining 65%, keeps that speed through the stop delay, and the spin-out inherits it through preserveSpeed, clamped between 8% and 100% of spinSpeed. anticipation:reel fires at the tease start; anticipation:reelEnd fires from _markLanded, just before spin:reelLanded.">
    <line x1={70} y1={240} x2={690} y2={240} class="guide-strong" />
    <line x1={70} y1={40} x2={70} y2={240} class="guide-strong" />
    <Label x={64} y={yOf(1) + 4} text="100%" anchor="end" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={64} y={yOf(0.3) + 4} text="30%" anchor="end" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={64} y={yOf(0.08) + 4} text="8%" anchor="end" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={64} y={yOf(0) + 6} text="0" anchor="end" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={22} y={150} text="speed / spinSpeed" anchor="middle" rotate={-90} />

    <line x1={70} y1={yOf(1)} x2={690} y2={yOf(1)} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray:4 3" />
    <line x1={70} y1={yOf(0.08)} x2={690} y2={yOf(0.08)} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray:4 3" />
    {[LX.drop, LX.hold, LX.delay, LX.out].map((x) => (
      <line x1={x} y1={58} x2={x} y2={240} class="guide" />
    ))}
    <path d={legacyPath} class="edge edge-primary" />

    <Label x={222} y={48} text="drop 35%, power2.out" anchor="middle" />
    <Label x={688} y={52} text="preserveSpeed cap" anchor="end" tone="faint" />
    <Label x={342} y={174} text="hold 65% at 30%" anchor="middle" tone="fg" weight={600} />
    <Label x={543} y={174} text="preserveSpeed" anchor="middle" tone="fg" weight={600} />
    <Label x={272} y={220} text="preserveSpeed floor" tone="faint" />

    {([
      [LX.spin, LX.drop, 'span-spin', 'spin'],
      [LX.drop, LX.hold, 'span-anticipation', 'tease'],
      [LX.hold, LX.delay, 'span-rest', 'stop delay'],
      [LX.delay, LX.out, 'span-stop', 'spin-out'],
      [LX.out, LX.end, 'span-bounce', 'bounce'],
    ] as const).map(([a, b, cls, name]) => (
      <>
        <rect x={a} y={252} width={b - a} height={14} rx={3} class={cls} />
        <Label x={(a + b) / 2} y={284} text={name} anchor="middle" />
      </>
    ))}
  </Diagram>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'tease', note: 'one cut step; a quicken ends it, a slam cancels it' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'stop.delay', note: 'runs after the tease at the tease speed' },
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'spinOut', note: 'preserveSpeed only when the tease played to its end and no quicken touched the reel' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    This is the <strong>legacy shape</strong>, the one you get with no
    <code>curve</code>: a target of <code>spinSpeed * 0.3</code>, reached over
    35% of the hold with <code>power2.out</code>, then held for the remaining
    65%. ADR 020 explains the ease choice: an ease-out applied to a speed puts
    the jerk spike, the moment the braking changes fastest, at the very start,
    which is where the player expects a brake to bite. Under the tween motion model that
    is a GSAP timeline followed by an empty tween; under the drive model it
    sets a target speed and waits out the duration.
  </p>

  <h2>Calling setAnticipation</h2>

  <p>
    <code>setAnticipation(reelIndices, options = 0)</code> takes the reels to
    tease and either a bare stagger value or an options object. Two rules
    about when to call it. It must come <strong>after</strong>
    <code>spin()</code>, because <code>spin()</code> wipes every anticipation
    setting, and <strong>before</strong> <code>setResult()</code>, so that a
    queued <code>requestSkip()</code> firing inside <code>setResult</code> can
    see the protection you set. Reels that this spin holds are dropped
    silently. If you pass functions for <code>curve</code> or
    <code>cells</code>, they are called right away, once per reel, with
    <code>(order, total)</code>, where <code>order</code> is the reel's
    position in your list; a function that returns nothing throws, and because
    they run once, a random choice or a counter inside them behaves
    predictably. <code>protect: true</code> is normalised to <code>'once'</code>.
  </p>

  <p>The options, with their defaults.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Option</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Type</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Default</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ['stagger', "number | number[] | 'sequential'", '0', 'offset between tease starts, by tease order, not reel index'],
            ['slowdown', '{ from?, to?, holdFrom?, holdTo? }', 'from 0.3, to = from, holdFrom 1, holdTo = holdFrom', 'linear interpolation across tease order'],
            ['protect', "boolean | 'once' | 'stepwise' | 'always'", 'false', 'what a skip() press may end; true means once'],
            ['duration', 'number (ms)', "profile anticipationDelay", 'the hold; a positive value keeps the tease alive on TURBO and SUPER_TURBO'],
            ['curve', 'AnticipationSegment[] | (order, total) => segments', 'none (legacy shape)', 'segments of { speed, duration, ease?, hold? }; ease defaults to power2.inOut'],
            ['cells', 'number | (order, total) => number', 'none', 'travel target in symbol pitches for the final leg, with duration as the backstop'],
          ].map(([k, t, d, m]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs">{k}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{t}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{d}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{m}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The options are validated at the call, so a mistake shows up where you made
    it rather than mid-spin. It throws if you give both <code>curve</code> and
    <code>slowdown</code> (they describe the same thing two ways), if
    <code>cells</code> is neither a function nor a positive number, if the
    <code>curve</code> is empty, or if any segment has a negative or NaN
    <code>speed</code>, a <code>duration</code> of 0 or less, or a negative
    <code>hold</code>. It warns (<code>anticipation-cells-unreachable</code>)
    if the final curve segment has speed 0 while <code>cells</code> is set,
    because a reel that is not moving can never reach a travel target.
  </p>

  <h2>When a reel decides to tease</h2>

  <p>
    The decision is made inside the reel's chain, after the group barrier.
    The controller works out the effective hold: the <code>duration</code> you
    passed, else the profile's <code>anticipationDelay</code>. A reel teases
    only if it is on the list, that hold is above zero, and no quicken has
    reached it yet. NORMAL holds 450 ms and TURBO 250 ms, so both tease;
    SUPER_TURBO's <code>anticipationDelay</code> is 0, so it never teases
    unless you pass a <code>duration</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    A reel that will tease first waits out its stagger offset at full speed.
    Then it joins the teasing set, tells the reel the tease is starting
    (<code>notifyAnticipationStart</code>, which lets a symbol relax its spin
    look, for example fading out a motion blur, through its
    <code>onReelAnticipationStart</code> hook), emits
    <code>anticipation:reel {'{ reelIndex, order, total }'}</code> and then
    <code>spin:stopping</code>, and runs the anticipation phase. Inside the
    phase there is a single step, the tease, and its length is
    <code>config.duration ?? step.duration ?? timing.anticipationDelay</code>;
    if that comes out at 0 or less the tease ends at once. When the step
    resolves the phase completes, and the controller creates the stop phase
    with <code>preserveSpeed</code> set if the tease played to its end and
    nothing quickened it.
  </p>

  <h2>The curve shape</h2>

  <p>
    When one brake-and-hold is not enough, a <code>curve</code> replaces the
    legacy shape with a list of segments the reel plays in order. Figure 2 is
    a three-segment example: a surge above full speed, a long crawl, and a
    final leg at 30%. Each segment eases to its target speed over its duration
    and then holds. Look at the last hold: with <code>cells</code> set it ends
    when the reel has travelled that many cells, so the reel always creeps the
    same visible distance no matter what speed it reached.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={296} caption="A curve shape with three segments. Each leg tweens speed to spinSpeed x seg.speed over seg.duration with power2.inOut and then holds; anticipation:segment fires at each leg start. With cells set, the final hold ends when the odometer has moved that many cells, or when max(hold, backstop) runs out.">
    <line x1={70} y1={240} x2={690} y2={240} class="guide-strong" />
    <line x1={70} y1={40} x2={70} y2={240} class="guide-strong" />
    {[0, 0.5, 1, 1.5].map((m) => (
      <>
        <line x1={70} y1={yC(m)} x2={690} y2={yC(m)} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray:4 3" />
        <Label x={64} y={yC(m) + 4} text={`${Math.round(m * 100)}%`} anchor="end" mono tone="faint" />
      </>
    ))}
    <path d={curvePath} class="edge edge-primary" />
    {segMarks.map((m, i) => (
      <>
        <circle cx={m.x} cy={yC(m.from)} r={5} class="span-bounce" />
        <rect x={m.x} y={252} width={m.xEnd - m.x} height={8} rx={2} class="span-anticipation" />
        <rect x={m.xEnd} y={252} width={m.xHold - m.xEnd} height={8} rx={2} class="span-rest" />
        <Label x={(m.x + m.xHold) / 2} y={280} text={`${m.seg.name} ${m.seg.speed}`} anchor="middle" mono />
      </>
    ))}
    <Label x={134} y={142} text="segment 0" anchor="end" mono />
    <Label x={segMarks[1].x} y={48} text="segment 1" anchor="middle" mono />
    <Label x={segMarks[2].x - 8} y={210} text="segment 2" anchor="end" mono />
    <Label x={105} y={108} text="full speed" anchor="middle" tone="faint" />
    <Edge from={[segMarks[2].xEnd, 170]} to={[segMarks[2].xHold, 170]} arrow="both" />
    <Label x={(segMarks[2].xEnd + segMarks[2].xHold) / 2} y={156} text="cells or backstop" anchor="middle" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    For each segment the phase emits
    <code>anticipation:segment {'{ reelIndex, index, total, speed, targetSpeed }'}</code>,
    tweens the speed to <code>spinSpeed * seg.speed</code> over
    <code>seg.duration</code> with <code>seg.ease</code> (default
    <code>power2.inOut</code>), holds for <code>seg.hold</code>, and moves on.
    Earlier legs always play in full; only the final leg can be shortened or
    stretched by a travel target. With <code>cells</code> the phase notes the
    reel's odometer when the final hold begins and keeps holding until the
    reel has moved that many cells, or until <code>max(hold, backstop)</code>
    has passed, where the backstop is the effective hold in milliseconds. On
    every update the phase ticks its steps and then runs that travel check.
  </p>

  <p>
    A press ends the phase cleanly whichever shape is running. For a slam or a
    quicken the phase kills its tween or delayed call and forces the reel back
    to full speed; under a quicken the step runner then cuts the tease step,
    which completes the phase, and the reel is <em>not</em> counted as having
    anticipated. Under the drive model a segment's <code>duration</code> is a
    time budget rather than a tween length, and if the drive misses the target
    by more than 0.5 the phase warns once (<code>anticipation-drive-budget</code>).
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    A <code>duration</code> is a time budget, so how far the reel travels
    during a tease depends on the speed curve: two reels with different
    slowdowns pass a different number of symbols. <code>cells</code> anchors
    the tease to travel instead, and it measures only the final leg, because
    arming the odometer at the start would let a surge leg burn through the
    budget before the crawl ever played. The time stays as a backstop because
    a reel at rest can never reach a travel target, and a tease that never
    ends is a hung spin.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Spacing out several teases</h2>

  <p>
    When two or three reels tease at once, starting them all in the same
    instant looks mechanical. The <code>stagger</code> option offsets their
    starts, and the offset is by <strong>tease order</strong>, the position in
    the list you passed, not by reel index. Figure 3 shows the three forms on
    three tease reels. In every panel the first reel never waits, and a
    waiting reel keeps spinning at full speed. Notice how much longer the
    third panel is: <code>'sequential'</code> waits for the previous tease
    reel to land completely, bounce included, before the next one starts.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={620} caption="The three stagger forms on three tease reels (tease order 0, 1, 2), on one time scale in ms. The offset is by tease order, the first reel never waits, and a waiting reel spins at full speed. 'sequential' waits for the previous tease reel's landing promise, so it is the slowest.">
    {[0, 500, 1000, 1500, 2000].map((t) => (
      <Label x={LANE_X0 + t * S} y={40} text={`${t}`} anchor="middle" mono tone="faint" />
    ))}
    {panels.map((p, pi) => {
      const py = PANEL_Y(pi);
      return (
        <>
          <Group x={20} y={py} w={680} h={PANEL_H} label={p.title} note={p.note} />
          {[0, 500, 1000, 1500, 2000].map((t) => (
            <line x1={LANE_X0 + t * S} y1={py + 36} x2={LANE_X0 + t * S} y2={py + 156} class="guide" />
          ))}
          {p.offsets.map((off, order) => {
            const y = py + 40 + order * 40;
            const tStart = off * S;
            const tEnd = (off + TEASE) * S;
            const sEnd = (off + TEASE + STOP) * S;
            return (
              <>
                <Label x={36} y={y + 21} text={`#${order}`} mono tone="faint" />
                <rect x={LANE_X0} y={y} width={LANE_W} height={32} rx={5} class="lane-bg" />
                {off > 0 && <rect x={LANE_X0} y={y + 4} width={tStart} height={24} rx={3} class="span-spin" />}
                <rect x={LANE_X0 + tStart} y={y + 4} width={tEnd - tStart} height={24} rx={3} class="span-anticipation" />
                <rect x={LANE_X0 + tEnd} y={y + 4} width={sEnd - tEnd} height={24} rx={3} class="span-stop" />
              </>
            );
          })}
        </>
      );
    })}
    {([
      ['span-spin', 'wait at full speed', 70],
      ['span-anticipation', 'tease 450 ms', 250],
      ['span-stop', 'stop delay 250 ms', 390],
    ] as const).map(([cls, label, x]) => (
      <>
        <rect x={x} y={594} width={14} height={13} rx={3} class={cls} />
        <Label x={x + 20} y={605} text={label} />
      </>
    ))}
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    A number gives <code>order * n</code> milliseconds, waited out with
    <code>setTimeout</code>. An array gives <code>arr[order] ?? 0</code>, so
    you can hand-place each start. <code>'sequential'</code> awaits the
    previous tease reel's landing promise. Those landing promises are created
    once per round at the start of <code>spin()</code>, not inside
    <code>setAnticipation</code>, so a waiter can never be handed a promise for
    a reel that has already landed. The
    <a href="/architecture/timeline/">timeline page</a> shows that
    <code>'sequential'</code> on two reels pushes the end of the spin from
    about 3.4 s to about 4.9 s.
  </p>

  <h2>Making later teases slower</h2>

  <p>
    A common feel is for each successive tease to be a little slower and a
    little longer than the last, so the tension builds. The
    <code>slowdown</code> option does that by interpolating linearly across
    tease order: <code>f</code> runs from 0 on the first tease reel to 1 on
    the last, the speed multiplier goes from <code>from</code> to
    <code>to</code>, and the hold multiplier from <code>holdFrom</code> to
    <code>holdTo</code>. Figure 4 works one example through.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={196} caption="slowdown interpolates linearly across tease order with f = order / (count - 1). This example uses { from: 0.5, to: 0.2, holdFrom: 1, holdTo: 1.6 } on three reels with a 450 ms base hold.">
    {orders.map((o) => (
      <Box x={20 + o.order * 232} y={20} w={216} h={78} title={`tease order ${o.order}`} mono={false} kind={o.order === 1 ? 'accent' : 'subtle'} sub={[
        `f = ${o.f.toFixed(2)}, speed x ${o.mult.toFixed(2)}`,
        `hold x ${o.hold.toFixed(2)} = ${o.duration} ms`,
      ]} />
    ))}
    <Label x={20} y={136} text={[
      'speedMultiplier = from + (to - from) * f',
      'holdMult = holdFrom + (holdTo - holdFrom) * f',
      'duration = base * holdMult',
    ]} mono tone="fg" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The defaults are <code>from</code> 0.3, <code>to</code> equal to
    <code>from</code>, <code>holdFrom</code> 1 and <code>holdTo</code> equal to
    <code>holdFrom</code>. The base hold is the
    <code>duration</code> you passed, else the profile's
    <code>anticipationDelay</code>; with a single tease reel <code>f</code> is
    0. The controller builds the per-reel phase config from these: with a
    curve it passes the resolved segments, the cells, and a
    <code>duration</code> that only decides whether the tease runs and serves
    as the travel backstop; with no slowdown it passes nothing extra so the
    phase defaults apply; with a slowdown it passes the two multipliers, and
    writes a <code>duration</code> only when the hold multiplier differs from
    1 or an override exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Protecting the tease from a press</h2>

  <p>
    A player who taps skip during a tease usually wants the boring columns to
    drop, not the exciting ones. <code>protect</code> says what a
    <code>skip()</code> or a queued <code>requestSkip()</code> press may end.
    <code>'once'</code> (or <code>true</code>) lets the first press land only
    the non-tease reels and the next press land the tease. <code>'stepwise'</code>
    releases one tease reel per later press, in tease order. <code>'always'</code>
    never lets a press end a tease, which leaves the skip stage at 1 for the
    rest of the round. <code>slamStop()</code> ignores all of it. Protection
    does nothing when the effective hold is 0 or less, and it reads the profile
    captured for this spin, not the live one. The press algorithm, the stage
    values and the full table are on the
    <a href="/architecture/skipping/">skipping page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Handing over to the stop</h2>

  <p>
    After a tease that played to its end, the reel is marked as having
    anticipated, and two things follow. First, its stop phase is created with
    <code>preserveSpeed</code>, so instead of returning to <code>spinSpeed</code>
    the spin-out keeps the current speed, clamped between 8% and 100% of
    <code>spinSpeed</code>; a surge above full speed is clamped at once, even
    under a drive. That is the creep. Second, every phase created afterwards
    for this reel sees <code>anticipated</code> in its context, so the
    profile's <code>whenAnticipated</code> sections apply to its timing and
    step settings; this is how a teased reel can get a longer, softer bounce
    (see <a href="/architecture/speed-profiles/">speed profiles</a>). A tease
    cut short by a quicken sets neither: the spin-out runs at full speed and
    <code>whenAnticipated</code> is ignored.
  </p>

  <p>
    In cascade mode the tease is a pure hold at speed 0, because a falling
    reel cannot crawl. <code>curve</code> and <code>cells</code> are dropped
    with a one-time warning, the speed multiplier is forced to 0, and after
    the tease the reel is halted and snapped before <code>cascade:place</code>
    runs.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    The creep only happens when the tease ran to the end. If a quicken press
    cuts it short, the stop phase gets no <code>preserveSpeed</code> and the
    reel snaps back to full speed for its spin-out, which can look like the
    tease never happened. If that matters to your game, protect the tease, or
    use a slam for the press so the reel lands at once instead.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Picking the reels from the grid</h2>

  <p>
    Most games tease "every reel after the one that completed the scatter
    count", and there is a helper for exactly that.
    <code>anticipationForScatters(grid, {'{ symbol, trigger = 2, mode = \'all-remaining\' }'})</code>,
    in <code>spin/anticipationRecipes.ts</code>, counts <code>symbol</code> in
    each reel's visible cells from left to right; the trigger reel is the first
    one where the running total reaches <code>trigger</code>. It returns the
    reels after that one: all of them for <code>'all-remaining'</code>, or only
    those that hold at least one <code>symbol</code> for
    <code>'scatter-only'</code>, and an empty list if the trigger is never
    reached. Pass the result straight to <code>setAnticipation</code>. Worked
    examples live under the <a href="/recipes/anticipation/">anticipation
    recipes</a>.
  </p>

